  "problem": "$\\operatorname{arcsin}\u2061(\\frac{1}{2})=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}\u2061x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{\u2218}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{\u2218}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{1}{2}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14036892/assets/ca39c4cde40b99fd40a11746a31398d82814268b2df706e3101ee88648c9e2d4.png)\n\nFrom the values in the first quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}\u2061({30}^{\u2218})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}\u2061(\\frac{1}{2})={30}^{\u2218}.\n$$",
